## Organizing Movies with Enums

A movie has a title, year, and genre. An enum ensures the genre is always one of the valid options.

### The Genre Enum

```xiom
enum Genre {
  Action,
  Comedy,
  Drama,
  SciFi,
  Horror,
}
```

### The Movie Struct

```xiom
type Movie = {
  title: Str;
  genre: Genre;
  year: Int;
}
```

### Creating Movies

```xiom
let alien = Movie{ title: "Alien"; genre: Genre.SciFi; year: 1979; };
let matrix = Movie{ title: "The Matrix"; genre: Genre.Action; year: 1999; };
```

### Filtering Classics

A function that checks if a movie is a classic (released before 2000):

```xiom
fn is_classic(m: Movie) -> Bool {
  return m.year < 2000;
}
```

### Using the Filter

```xiom
is_classic(alien);   // true  (1979 < 2000)
is_classic(matrix);  // true  (1999 < 2000)

let recent = Movie{ title: "Dune"; genre: Genre.SciFi; year: 2021; };
is_classic(recent);  // false
```

### Enums Prevent Typos

Without an enum, you'd write `"SciFi"` and risk `"Sci-Fi"` or `"sci-fi"`. The enum guarantees only valid variants exist � the compiler catches mistakes.

